If you've been using Ruby for a while, you've likely come across [`Tempfile`](https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/Tempfile.html) in your projects. In short, it creates a temporary file in the system's `/tmp` directory. From there, you can pass it as an argument to other methods just like a regular file (I mostly see this used in image manipulation gems like [`mini_magick`](https://github.com/minimagick/minimagick/blob/master/lib/mini_magick/utilities.rb)). If you pass a block, the file is automatically deleted after execution. If not, you'll need to close and unlink it manually. Keep in mind that `Tempfile.create` returns an instance of `File`, not `Tempfile`.
What you might have missed is that Ruby also provides [`Dir.mktmpdir`](https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/Dir.html#method-c-mktmpdir), which creates a temporary directory (also in `/tmp`). This is especially useful when writing integration tests where you need to generate multiple temporary files for downstream steps. `Dir.mktmpdir` returns the path to the temporary folder, allowing you to create files inside it using standard methods like `File.write`.

Do you use temporary files and directories in your projects? In what scenarios has this feature been most helpful?

```ruby
require 'tempfile'

f = Tempfile.create
f.write('Some text')
f.flush
f.path # "/tmp/20261007-238355-ug95xp"
f.close
File.unlink(f)

Tempfile.create do |f|
  f.sync = true
  f.write('Some text')

  # work with file
end


require 'tmpdir'

dir = Dir.mktmpdir
f = File.join(dir, 'f.txt') # /tmp/d20261007-457697-nj2gb8/f.txt
File.write(f, 'Some data')
FileUtils.remove_entry(dir)

Dir.mktmpdir do |dir|
  f = File.join(dir, 'f.txt')
  File.write(f, 'Some data')

  # work with file and folder
end
```
